  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14247769/assets/c62f51c2b029d6b1e24e7f30603c5f34ab5d70d96ed84c5c74de15d4e2e6358e.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $∠D?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that $△ABC$ is an equilateral triangle. Consequently, all of the interior angles must be equal to ${60}^{∘}:$ \n\n$$\nm∠A=m∠B=m∠ABC={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nAlso, since $△CDB$ is an isosceles triangle, the base angles must have equal measure:\n\n$$\nm∠CBD=m∠D\n$$\n\nNotice also that $∠ACB$ and $∠DCB$ are supplementary, therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠ACB+m∠DCB & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {60}^{∘}+m∠DCB & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠DCB & ={120}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Finally, we calculate $m∠D$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠D+m∠DCB+m∠CBD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D+{120}^{∘}+m∠D & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠D & ={60}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D & ={30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
